(Title) Tj
T*
(On-site hepatitis C care reduces injection drug use and related risk in people who inject) Tj
T*
(drugs: findings from an accessible care model trial) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Structural barriers significantly limit hepatitis C treatment access among people who) Tj
T*
(inject drugs, yet evidence on innovative care-delivery models remains limited. A) Tj
T*
(randomized 1:1 assignment trial enrolled 165 HCV RNA-positive individuals with recent) Tj
T*
(injection drug use and randomized participants to either on-site hepatitis C care \(n=82\)) Tj
T*
(delivered directly at a harm-reduction program, or standard referral pathways \(n=83\). The) Tj
T*
(on-site care model provided clinical assessment, treatment initiation, psychosocial) Tj
T*
(support, and medication management without requiring external facility referral.) Tj
T*
(Participants attended structured follow-up interviews at 3, 6, 9, and 12 months, with) Tj
T*
(standardized assessments of injection patterns, high-risk injection practices, and) Tj
T*
(medication-assisted treatment engagement. By 12-month follow-up, on-site care participants) Tj
T*
(reported statistically significantly greater reductions in injection drug use frequency) Tj
T*
(compared with standard-referral participants. High-risk injection behaviors also declined) Tj
T*
(more substantially in the accessible-care arm. Rates of opioid-use-disorder treatment) Tj
T*
(engagement appeared similar between study arms. These findings demonstrate that removing) Tj
T*
(structural and administrative barriers to hepatitis C care can meaningfully reduce) Tj
T*
(injection-related behaviors and may amplify public-health impact in affected populations.) Tj
